WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:06.300
&gt;&gt; Good afternoon everyone.

00:00:06.301 --> 00:00:07.430
My name is Tanuja Ganu,

00:00:07.431 --> 00:00:10.070
and I'm co-founder
and CEO of DataGlen.

00:00:10.071 --> 00:00:11.880
Today I'm going to
present some of

00:00:11.881 --> 00:00:13.260
the work that we have been

00:00:13.261 --> 00:00:16.285
doing as part of the workshop
with my teams Siddhart,

00:00:16.286 --> 00:00:17.880
Mayuri, Prabhu and Subham as

00:00:17.881 --> 00:00:20.225
part of who helped
me in the workshop.

00:00:20.226 --> 00:00:22.410
So, I'll talk about

00:00:22.411 --> 00:00:24.950
multiple problems that
we have been handling.

00:00:24.951 --> 00:00:26.430
The overall objective is

00:00:26.431 --> 00:00:28.710
improving the yield
of energy resources,

00:00:28.711 --> 00:00:32.310
distributed energy resources
using AI-driven approach.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:35.535
So, quick context
about the problem.

00:00:35.536 --> 00:00:39.480
So, as we know the energy
vertical is moving from

00:00:39.481 --> 00:00:42.770
decentralized generation
and distributed

00:00:42.771 --> 00:00:44.360
consumption to
distributed generation

00:00:44.361 --> 00:00:46.750
and distributed
consumption phenomenon.

00:00:46.751 --> 00:00:50.540
Where there are lot of
distributed energy resources in

00:00:50.541 --> 00:00:53.525
the form of solar or
electric vehicles storage

00:00:53.526 --> 00:00:54.560
which are coming in place,

00:00:54.561 --> 00:00:56.690
and that's adding
a lot of complexity to

00:00:56.691 --> 00:00:58.080
the entire network of the way

00:00:58.081 --> 00:01:00.185
we generate and consume energy.

00:01:00.186 --> 00:01:03.620
So, as effect of this there
are different types of

00:01:03.621 --> 00:01:04.670
equipments which are getting

00:01:04.671 --> 00:01:07.130
connected in the entire system,

00:01:07.131 --> 00:01:09.975
like solar inverters,
battery inverters,

00:01:09.976 --> 00:01:12.270
there could be electric vehicles.

00:01:12.271 --> 00:01:16.490
There are different
interesting business models

00:01:16.491 --> 00:01:18.140
and economical problems which

00:01:18.141 --> 00:01:20.130
are being generated
as part of this.

00:01:20.131 --> 00:01:25.290
So, what we do at
DataGlen presenters,

00:01:25.291 --> 00:01:27.500
we do manage and monitor

00:01:27.501 --> 00:01:30.660
different forms of
distributed energy resources.

00:01:30.661 --> 00:01:32.350
So, as of today, we have

00:01:32.351 --> 00:01:34.100
400 different solar sites
which are being

00:01:34.101 --> 00:01:38.144
managed in India which
includes the metro stations,

00:01:38.145 --> 00:01:42.765
hospitals, and other
small and large plants.

00:01:42.766 --> 00:01:44.590
As well, we are doing
a pilot project

00:01:44.591 --> 00:01:47.150
in Australia with
Australian utility,

00:01:47.151 --> 00:01:49.040
where it's a combination
of solar and

00:01:49.041 --> 00:01:51.235
storage for
200 residential homes.

00:01:51.236 --> 00:01:53.870
So, as part of the workshop

00:01:53.871 --> 00:01:56.735
we wanted to try out
some real-world challenges

00:01:56.736 --> 00:01:59.959
that we are encountering by
using some other technologies

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:03.170
which have been developed by
Microsoft team previously,

00:02:03.171 --> 00:02:06.535
and wanted to see how
that fits in use cases.

00:02:06.536 --> 00:02:08.170
So, these are
the typical challenges.

00:02:08.171 --> 00:02:10.445
So, this is where
the setup looks like.

00:02:10.446 --> 00:02:12.680
So, there are different
types of equipment

00:02:12.681 --> 00:02:14.920
including inverters,
battery storage,

00:02:14.921 --> 00:02:16.005
different types of sensors,

00:02:16.006 --> 00:02:18.155
which give additional contexts
and permission like

00:02:18.156 --> 00:02:21.195
irradiation sensor temperature
sensor energy meter.

00:02:21.196 --> 00:02:23.210
Typically, at
one particular site,

00:02:23.211 --> 00:02:24.980
if I'm considering
let's say Metro site

00:02:24.981 --> 00:02:27.230
or Hospital as
one particular site,

00:02:27.231 --> 00:02:28.580
there will be one data logger or

00:02:28.581 --> 00:02:31.545
DataGlen gateway which is
a single board Linux computer,

00:02:31.546 --> 00:02:32.750
which is connecting with these

00:02:32.751 --> 00:02:34.070
different types of equipment

00:02:34.071 --> 00:02:37.145
and doing data collection
and analytics Edge.

00:02:37.146 --> 00:02:39.660
It's pushing the data
in the DataGlen cloud,

00:02:39.661 --> 00:02:42.410
and basically doing bunch

00:02:42.411 --> 00:02:45.605
of other portfolio level
analytics at that.

00:02:45.606 --> 00:02:47.110
But typical challenges of

00:02:47.111 --> 00:02:49.450
IoT intermittent network
connectivity so,

00:02:49.451 --> 00:02:51.615
most of these sites are
at remote locations.

00:02:51.616 --> 00:02:53.620
Many a times we
observed that there is

00:02:53.621 --> 00:02:56.610
disconnectivity of network
for hours or days at a time.

00:02:56.611 --> 00:02:58.660
We cannot rely on

00:02:58.661 --> 00:03:01.880
cloud-based Analytics for
low latency control responses.

00:03:01.881 --> 00:03:04.150
So, there are interesting use
cases where we really

00:03:04.151 --> 00:03:06.490
need a low latency
response and we

00:03:06.491 --> 00:03:08.980
cannot expect the data to be sent

00:03:08.981 --> 00:03:10.270
from the Edge to the cloud

00:03:10.271 --> 00:03:11.980
and made the decision
on the cloud.

00:03:11.981 --> 00:03:13.690
So, those are
the kind of use cases

00:03:13.691 --> 00:03:15.775
we are going to talk
about over here.

00:03:15.776 --> 00:03:18.655
So, the first problem that
we wanted to tackle was

00:03:18.656 --> 00:03:21.775
intraday forecasting for
local generation and demand.

00:03:21.776 --> 00:03:23.540
So, why this problem
is important.

00:03:23.541 --> 00:03:24.685
So, we want to look at

00:03:24.686 --> 00:03:27.780
optimal utilization of
distributed energy resources.

00:03:27.781 --> 00:03:31.390
So, take an example of a house
where there is a solar,

00:03:31.391 --> 00:03:32.605
there is a battery storage and

00:03:32.606 --> 00:03:34.280
there is a time of use pricing.

00:03:34.281 --> 00:03:36.070
So, solar regenerating energy at

00:03:36.071 --> 00:03:40.565
a typical time period of lets
say, 6:00 AM to 6:00 PM.

00:03:40.566 --> 00:03:42.550
There are constraints on
the battery storage of

00:03:42.551 --> 00:03:44.810
how much amount of charging
and discharging can happen.

00:03:44.811 --> 00:03:47.815
There are time of use pricing

00:03:47.816 --> 00:03:49.600
so there are different hours of

00:03:49.601 --> 00:03:51.790
the day which has
different pricing.

00:03:51.791 --> 00:03:53.410
So, if we want to have

00:03:53.411 --> 00:03:55.240
the policy to basically

00:03:55.241 --> 00:03:57.215
reduce the overall bill
for a consumer,

00:03:57.216 --> 00:03:58.555
what we need to do is

00:03:58.556 --> 00:04:00.610
accurate forecasting
of local generation,

00:04:00.611 --> 00:04:02.410
accurate forecasting
of local demand,

00:04:02.411 --> 00:04:05.020
and then optimization to
comply with the policy.

00:04:05.021 --> 00:04:07.490
So, typically what we

00:04:07.491 --> 00:04:09.590
do is we do have
day-ahead forecasting,

00:04:09.591 --> 00:04:11.885
which is based on
the historical data

00:04:11.886 --> 00:04:13.330
from the same location.

00:04:13.331 --> 00:04:14.525
We also take into account

00:04:14.526 --> 00:04:17.525
multiple third party weather
forecasting services

00:04:17.526 --> 00:04:19.880
to do the forecasting
for the next 24 hours.

00:04:19.881 --> 00:04:22.455
How much amount of energy
that would be generated.

00:04:22.456 --> 00:04:23.870
But many a times due to

00:04:23.871 --> 00:04:26.270
various different
reasons including

00:04:26.271 --> 00:04:28.900
the weather forecasting service
itself is not able to

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:31.830
accurate the forecast of
the weather correctly.

00:04:31.831 --> 00:04:33.080
Or there are issues with

00:04:33.081 --> 00:04:35.620
the equipment during the runtime,

00:04:35.621 --> 00:04:39.280
or there could be demand spikes
because various reasons.

00:04:39.281 --> 00:04:42.085
There could be changes during

00:04:42.086 --> 00:04:43.580
the day there would be changes

00:04:43.581 --> 00:04:47.810
in the actual forecasting
of a generation and demand.

00:04:47.811 --> 00:04:49.340
So, we wanted to see how we

00:04:49.341 --> 00:04:52.835
can do the intraday
divisions into

00:04:52.836 --> 00:04:55.685
generation and forecasting
on Edge without going back

00:04:55.686 --> 00:04:58.905
to the cloud computing.

00:04:58.906 --> 00:05:03.950
So, we looked at this problem
as the regression problem,

00:05:03.951 --> 00:05:07.765
where we are taking into
account the information from

00:05:07.766 --> 00:05:09.725
the day-ahead forecasting for

00:05:09.726 --> 00:05:12.790
the next few slots as
well the let's say 10:30,

00:05:12.791 --> 00:05:14.540
I want to focus how much energy

00:05:14.541 --> 00:05:16.430
that would be generated
for next two hours.

00:05:16.431 --> 00:05:19.020
So, I have information
of actual generation

00:05:19.021 --> 00:05:22.095
for last whatever
three or four hours.

00:05:22.096 --> 00:05:23.585
We do have information about

00:05:23.586 --> 00:05:25.970
actual generation which has
happened during those hours,

00:05:25.971 --> 00:05:27.890
and for each of the look-ahead we

00:05:27.891 --> 00:05:29.965
have independent
regression models.

00:05:29.966 --> 00:05:33.370
So, here we basically we have

00:05:33.371 --> 00:05:35.080
used existing algorithms

00:05:35.081 --> 00:05:36.840
which includes gradient boosting,

00:05:36.841 --> 00:05:38.410
Random Forest, and other things,

00:05:38.411 --> 00:05:40.360
but we also wanted
to evaluate how

00:05:40.361 --> 00:05:41.800
the Bonsai and ProtoNN regression

00:05:41.801 --> 00:05:44.065
models work in this case.

00:05:44.066 --> 00:05:45.880
So, the Bonsai and

00:05:45.881 --> 00:05:48.295
ProtoNN models which
are built by MSR team.

00:05:48.296 --> 00:05:50.170
Those were mainly
built initially for

00:05:50.171 --> 00:05:52.150
multi-class and
multilevel classification.

00:05:52.151 --> 00:05:54.489
Those were not evaluated

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:56.820
or tested on the
regression models.

00:05:56.821 --> 00:05:58.780
So, we basically tried
those models for

00:05:58.781 --> 00:05:59.830
the regression problems in

00:05:59.831 --> 00:06:02.240
this case and for
each look-ahead window.

00:06:02.241 --> 00:06:04.525
So, over 15 minute look-ahead
we have one model,

00:06:04.526 --> 00:06:06.725
for 30 minute look-ahead
we have different model.

00:06:06.726 --> 00:06:09.400
One interesting point to
observe here is we had

00:06:09.401 --> 00:06:12.670
tried LSTM and other
approaches before.

00:06:12.671 --> 00:06:15.770
But the issue with the LSTM
model is if you are doing

00:06:15.771 --> 00:06:17.320
the immediate look-ahead it gives

00:06:17.321 --> 00:06:19.070
you good prediction accuracy,

00:06:19.071 --> 00:06:22.010
but let's say if you want to
look-ahead after one hour,

00:06:22.011 --> 00:06:24.240
you have to fill
in the information

00:06:24.241 --> 00:06:26.150
in between with
your predicted information,

00:06:26.151 --> 00:06:27.980
and if there is a error it just

00:06:27.981 --> 00:06:29.660
propagates and
exponentially increases

00:06:29.661 --> 00:06:30.770
the error in the problem.

00:06:30.771 --> 00:06:32.750
So, we wanted to have
the independent model so

00:06:32.751 --> 00:06:35.105
that that problem is
tackled over here.

00:06:35.106 --> 00:06:36.680
So, these are some of the results

00:06:36.681 --> 00:06:39.155
from Bonsai and ProtoNN,

00:06:39.156 --> 00:06:41.750
along with the existing
approaches that we had studied.

00:06:41.751 --> 00:06:44.270
So, on left-hand side
we are showing

00:06:44.271 --> 00:06:47.250
the root-mean-squared error
on each of the slot.

00:06:47.251 --> 00:06:49.230
So, x-axis, it shows

00:06:49.231 --> 00:06:51.530
slots for different
look-ahead windows.

00:06:51.531 --> 00:06:56.330
So, one slot look-ahead two
slot look-ahead store liquid.

00:06:56.331 --> 00:06:58.040
As we can expect
for the immediate

00:06:58.041 --> 00:07:00.580
look-ahead the
root-mean-square-error is low,

00:07:00.581 --> 00:07:03.090
so the accuracy is better
for immediate slots.

00:07:03.091 --> 00:07:05.400
As we go along for

00:07:05.401 --> 00:07:07.805
these slots accuracy
slightly decreases,

00:07:07.806 --> 00:07:11.320
but interestingly the
root-mean-squared error Bonsai and

00:07:11.321 --> 00:07:12.710
ProtoNN is lower than

00:07:12.711 --> 00:07:15.765
Random Forest and gradient
boosting in this case.

00:07:15.766 --> 00:07:20.000
It's consistent or comparable
in the case of six,

00:07:20.001 --> 00:07:22.550
seven and eight hours
look-ahead time slots as well.

00:07:22.551 --> 00:07:26.100
If we look at the model sizes
on the right-hand side,

00:07:26.101 --> 00:07:28.050
this is the log scale.

00:07:28.051 --> 00:07:30.735
So, in case of
Bonsai and ProtoNN,

00:07:30.736 --> 00:07:34.535
it's using 4.5 kB of model size,

00:07:34.536 --> 00:07:37.400
whereas the XG Boost was

00:07:37.401 --> 00:07:40.555
using close to 500 KB
of model size and

00:07:40.556 --> 00:07:43.550
Random Forest was
using close to I think

00:07:43.551 --> 00:07:47.000
two megabyte of model size
for the same kind of results.

00:07:47.001 --> 00:07:49.910
So, it gives significant
reduction in the model size

00:07:49.911 --> 00:07:53.440
while keeping
the accuracies consistent.

00:07:53.441 --> 00:07:56.630
So, in this case we use
60-days training data and

00:07:56.631 --> 00:07:59.855
10 days test data over
multiple different inverters,

00:07:59.856 --> 00:08:02.500
multiple different locations
and did the results.

00:08:02.501 --> 00:08:05.560
Visual dimension relatively
also around 35 seconds.

00:08:05.561 --> 00:08:07.800
So, here is a quick demo on this.

00:08:07.801 --> 00:08:09.170
So, we are looking at

00:08:09.171 --> 00:08:10.550
individual look-ahead slots with

00:08:10.551 --> 00:08:11.920
different models which are built.

00:08:11.921 --> 00:08:13.640
So, the training is
done on the clouds,

00:08:13.641 --> 00:08:14.720
the moderates are deployed on

00:08:14.721 --> 00:08:16.690
the Raspberry Pi which
is sitting there.

00:08:16.691 --> 00:08:19.030
So, this is the box.

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:22.910
Basically the evaluations
of the results

00:08:22.911 --> 00:08:26.670
are performed on
multiple different locations.

00:08:26.671 --> 00:08:28.400
So, I'll just go to the demo link

00:08:28.401 --> 00:08:31.285
to give a quick demo
of how it works.

00:08:31.286 --> 00:08:32.430
So, basically it's showing

00:08:32.431 --> 00:08:34.660
that we do have
a day-ahead forecasting,

00:08:34.661 --> 00:08:37.160
and depending upon
the actual generation,

00:08:37.161 --> 00:08:41.260
the next eight slots forecasts
keeps on changing.

00:08:41.261 --> 00:08:45.020
So, on the days where
either because of

00:08:45.021 --> 00:08:49.160
the equipment failure or
because of the weather changes,

00:08:49.161 --> 00:08:51.320
if there are variations
which are needed then it

00:08:51.321 --> 00:08:53.550
would accurately
predict the NXP hours,

00:08:53.551 --> 00:08:54.965
and the optimization,

00:08:54.966 --> 00:08:58.610
downstream optimization would
basically work accordingly.

00:08:58.611 --> 00:09:01.530
So, basically, till
the end of the day it

00:09:01.531 --> 00:09:04.995
would keep on changing
the prediction accuracies.

00:09:04.996 --> 00:09:07.550
So, the advantage over here is,

00:09:07.551 --> 00:09:08.945
the models are compressed

00:09:08.946 --> 00:09:11.935
so it can easily fit on
the lower footprint,

00:09:11.936 --> 00:09:14.130
and provide the fast inference.

00:09:14.131 --> 00:09:15.710
So, it's tacking
around 10 milliseconds

00:09:15.711 --> 00:09:18.460
of time for the inference
in this case.

00:09:18.530 --> 00:09:21.860
So, the second problem that
we wanted to look at is

00:09:21.861 --> 00:09:23.090
predictive maintenance
for different

00:09:23.091 --> 00:09:25.045
types of energy equipment.

00:09:25.046 --> 00:09:27.140
So, based on the study,

00:09:27.141 --> 00:09:28.685
there are different types of

00:09:28.686 --> 00:09:31.100
maintenance issues
or costs which are

00:09:31.101 --> 00:09:33.575
associated with different
equipment in the solar plants.

00:09:33.576 --> 00:09:36.910
So, according to EPRI study
it shows that 50 percent of

00:09:36.911 --> 00:09:41.130
the costs on the O&amp;M are
associated with the inverters.

00:09:41.131 --> 00:09:43.665
So, we wanted to look at
inverters as equipment and

00:09:43.666 --> 00:09:48.665
how the downtimes issues which
are because of inverters.

00:09:48.666 --> 00:09:50.150
So, this is the data collected by

00:09:50.151 --> 00:09:52.110
our own monitoring system.

00:09:52.111 --> 00:09:54.065
So, there are
two manufacturers A and B.

00:09:54.066 --> 00:09:56.970
So, for manufacture A
we have a 81 inverters,

00:09:56.971 --> 00:09:59.265
and the mean downtime is

00:09:59.266 --> 00:10:01.650
two percent in the manufacture A,

00:10:01.651 --> 00:10:04.130
whereas in manufacture
B it's around 4.52

00:10:04.131 --> 00:10:06.980
percent and there is
a long tail on both the cases.

00:10:06.981 --> 00:10:08.570
So, it's important
that we identify

00:10:08.571 --> 00:10:10.790
the issues for fixing
the downtime as well

00:10:10.791 --> 00:10:12.740
as for the warranty
enforcement cases so that

00:10:12.741 --> 00:10:15.450
the manufacturer can
replace in case of issues.

00:10:15.451 --> 00:10:18.530
So, here actually
the problem statement

00:10:18.531 --> 00:10:20.540
is alert the engineer
at least two to four

00:10:20.541 --> 00:10:22.775
hours in advance before
the actual downtime

00:10:22.776 --> 00:10:25.335
by reducing the number
of false alarms.

00:10:25.336 --> 00:10:27.980
So, for every individual
inverters we have a bunch

00:10:27.981 --> 00:10:31.320
of parameters that we get from
the individual inverters,

00:10:31.321 --> 00:10:33.965
which include different
voltages, currents,

00:10:33.966 --> 00:10:38.510
and temperature, and
other parameters.

00:10:38.511 --> 00:10:42.015
So, looking into this
we actually extended-

00:10:42.016 --> 00:10:45.495
Use approach which is
being used by MSR team for

00:10:45.496 --> 00:10:47.740
looking at predictive maintenance

00:10:47.741 --> 00:10:49.320
for other industrial
applications.

00:10:49.321 --> 00:10:51.975
So here, we are looking at 120,

00:10:51.976 --> 00:10:55.125
so two hours window and
sliding off 15 minutes.

00:10:55.126 --> 00:10:57.040
So, we're trying to look

00:10:57.041 --> 00:10:59.255
ahead in two ours,
so what we do is,

00:10:59.256 --> 00:11:01.535
we do a feature
sampling to identify

00:11:01.536 --> 00:11:05.395
that taking these individual
data points in case.

00:11:05.396 --> 00:11:09.925
So, let say if there is
a downtime at 11:10,

00:11:09.926 --> 00:11:12.960
so we consider a two-hour
window before that,

00:11:12.961 --> 00:11:15.830
and try to check whether
in this two-hour window,

00:11:15.831 --> 00:11:17.410
are we able to predict
that there is going to be

00:11:17.411 --> 00:11:19.435
a downtime after
two hours or four hours.

00:11:19.436 --> 00:11:21.060
So, we have done
different types of

00:11:21.061 --> 00:11:22.655
feature engineering to do

00:11:22.656 --> 00:11:24.360
extracting statistical
features and

00:11:24.361 --> 00:11:26.515
FFT features along
the sliding window of

00:11:26.516 --> 00:11:30.000
120 minutes and stride of 15.

00:11:30.001 --> 00:11:32.310
These features can be efficiently

00:11:32.311 --> 00:11:35.125
computed on the edge
using online algorithms.

00:11:35.126 --> 00:11:36.950
The challenge was data is very

00:11:36.951 --> 00:11:38.840
skewed or imbalance
so we had to do

00:11:38.841 --> 00:11:42.920
downsampling on the positive
or the right data so that

00:11:42.921 --> 00:11:44.740
we are able to take

00:11:44.741 --> 00:11:47.990
equal amount of the negative
and positive examples.

00:11:47.991 --> 00:11:50.900
These are some of
the early results.

00:11:50.901 --> 00:11:52.940
I think we need to do
a lot more experiments on this,

00:11:52.941 --> 00:11:56.850
but these are the early results
where we're able to get

00:11:56.851 --> 00:11:59.060
a 90 percent recall while keeping

00:11:59.061 --> 00:12:01.440
the false alarm rate
at 20 percent.

00:12:01.441 --> 00:12:02.990
So by 90 percent,

00:12:02.991 --> 00:12:05.550
this is equivalent to 97.4 hours

00:12:05.551 --> 00:12:08.910
of downtime that can be avoided
or proactively addressed.

00:12:08.911 --> 00:12:12.540
Since the models, which are
used decision-based models.

00:12:12.541 --> 00:12:14.155
The model is interpretable,

00:12:14.156 --> 00:12:16.410
so it helps in troubleshooting

00:12:16.411 --> 00:12:18.465
as well as for
warranty enforcement.

00:12:18.466 --> 00:12:21.100
The last thing that
I wanted to show

00:12:21.101 --> 00:12:23.645
was the third problem
in solar case.

00:12:23.646 --> 00:12:25.675
Most of the inefficiencies
are because of

00:12:25.676 --> 00:12:27.050
the soiling or the dust

00:12:27.051 --> 00:12:28.480
which gets accumulated
on the panel.

00:12:28.481 --> 00:12:31.150
So, that basically adds
to a significant amount

00:12:31.151 --> 00:12:32.450
of inefficiencies in the amount

00:12:32.451 --> 00:12:34.140
of energy that can be generated.

00:12:34.141 --> 00:12:36.200
So, this is the data across

00:12:36.201 --> 00:12:38.840
230 inverters from
our own dataset.

00:12:38.841 --> 00:12:43.040
So, as we see that
the panels are not clean.

00:12:43.041 --> 00:12:45.440
After three weeks, it gets
close to eight percent

00:12:45.441 --> 00:12:48.125
of reduction in
overall energy generation.

00:12:48.126 --> 00:12:52.570
So, it basically accounts
to eight percent of,

00:12:52.571 --> 00:12:54.400
close to eight percent
of soiling losses.

00:12:54.401 --> 00:12:56.405
So again, we wanted to model

00:12:56.406 --> 00:12:59.080
this problem as the
regression problem.

00:12:59.081 --> 00:13:00.550
So, here what we
are trying to do is

00:13:00.551 --> 00:13:02.715
a retrofit on how much amount of

00:13:02.716 --> 00:13:03.890
energy that should have been

00:13:03.891 --> 00:13:06.435
generated given
the observable parameters.

00:13:06.436 --> 00:13:09.490
So, the observable parameters
are solar radiation,

00:13:09.491 --> 00:13:11.645
module temperature,
ambient temperature,

00:13:11.646 --> 00:13:13.230
and the time of day.

00:13:13.231 --> 00:13:14.870
We're trying to predict

00:13:14.871 --> 00:13:15.750
how much amount of energy

00:13:15.751 --> 00:13:16.870
that should have been generated,

00:13:16.871 --> 00:13:19.375
and looking at
the residual analysis.

00:13:19.376 --> 00:13:21.350
We do have a patent application

00:13:21.351 --> 00:13:22.780
submitted on this already.

00:13:22.781 --> 00:13:25.180
So, we look at the residual
patterns and there are

00:13:25.181 --> 00:13:26.450
different types of
losses which are

00:13:26.451 --> 00:13:28.280
exhibit in different
types of patterns.

00:13:28.281 --> 00:13:29.960
So, if in case of soiling losses,

00:13:29.961 --> 00:13:32.665
we see that instead of
residuals being random,

00:13:32.666 --> 00:13:34.120
they have very nice pattern.

00:13:34.121 --> 00:13:35.690
When cleaning is done,

00:13:35.691 --> 00:13:38.060
it goes positive and overtime
it comes negative.

00:13:38.061 --> 00:13:39.640
Based on that, we can also

00:13:39.641 --> 00:13:42.500
quantify how much is
the soiling losses exactly.

00:13:42.501 --> 00:13:46.480
So, we do have existing gradient

00:13:46.481 --> 00:13:47.720
boosting regression models which

00:13:47.721 --> 00:13:50.480
are supplied on the edge,

00:13:50.481 --> 00:13:54.055
which is getting
close to 20 KB of model sizes.

00:13:54.056 --> 00:13:55.830
We do experiments on

00:13:55.831 --> 00:13:57.765
Bonsai and ProtoNN and
we wanted to see if

00:13:57.766 --> 00:13:59.430
those can be applied
to further get

00:13:59.431 --> 00:14:01.630
the reduction in model sizes.

00:14:01.631 --> 00:14:05.390
Interestingly, we have not
reached the accuracies

00:14:05.391 --> 00:14:08.630
of what gradient boosting

00:14:08.631 --> 00:14:11.880
and random forest are giving
using Bonsai and ProtoNN,

00:14:11.881 --> 00:14:13.990
and we did a lot of
variations on those,

00:14:13.991 --> 00:14:16.655
and try to understand why
those are not reaching there.

00:14:16.656 --> 00:14:18.310
So, here's a quick observation

00:14:18.311 --> 00:14:20.940
where the actual data
looks like this.

00:14:20.941 --> 00:14:23.185
So, there are two features
which are most important,

00:14:23.186 --> 00:14:24.915
X-axis is the radiation,

00:14:24.916 --> 00:14:26.560
Y-axis is model temperature and

00:14:26.561 --> 00:14:28.500
Z-axis is the actual power.

00:14:28.501 --> 00:14:32.810
So, there is a linear
correlation or there are

00:14:32.811 --> 00:14:36.150
good correlations between
some high-level attributes

00:14:36.151 --> 00:14:37.720
where there is
a scatter which is seen

00:14:37.721 --> 00:14:39.285
in the original data,

00:14:39.286 --> 00:14:40.700
which is being picked up by

00:14:40.701 --> 00:14:43.355
ensemble models and
by weak classifiers.

00:14:43.356 --> 00:14:46.910
But whereas Bonsai being
a strong predictor,

00:14:46.911 --> 00:14:50.430
it's able to predict the
linear function correctly but

00:14:50.431 --> 00:14:52.280
not beyond the scatter

00:14:52.281 --> 00:14:54.080
which is predicted by
the weak classifies,

00:14:54.081 --> 00:14:55.395
it start picking up those.

00:14:55.396 --> 00:14:58.520
So, it reaches 196 of r

00:14:58.521 --> 00:15:01.725
squared within one depth
tree and very fast.

00:15:01.726 --> 00:15:05.110
But from that 0.96 to.98,
it's not able to reach.

00:15:05.111 --> 00:15:08.075
&gt;&gt; Is it because Bonsai
and ProtoNN are more

00:15:08.076 --> 00:15:09.530
geared towards classification

00:15:09.531 --> 00:15:11.850
problems rather than regression?

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:15.220
&gt;&gt; I think this is mainly because

00:15:15.221 --> 00:15:18.120
of the weak classifiers.

00:15:18.121 --> 00:15:20.330
So, one thing is definitely

00:15:20.331 --> 00:15:22.410
in classification these kind

00:15:22.411 --> 00:15:24.265
of cases would be
directly avoided.

00:15:24.266 --> 00:15:27.150
These problems would not be
there in classification.

00:15:27.151 --> 00:15:30.185
We first time tried those
on regression problems.

00:15:30.186 --> 00:15:31.970
But it is fitting well on

00:15:31.971 --> 00:15:34.510
our first regression problem
where there are,

00:15:34.511 --> 00:15:37.250
I think, such kind of scatters.

00:15:37.251 --> 00:15:39.750
So, I think there is
a very peculiarity of

00:15:39.751 --> 00:15:41.410
this problem where there are

00:15:41.411 --> 00:15:43.390
two features which
are very dominant,

00:15:43.391 --> 00:15:45.695
and there are other
features which have impact,

00:15:45.696 --> 00:15:49.030
but those other features
are not getting captured

00:15:49.031 --> 00:15:50.730
in some sense that these kinds of

00:15:50.731 --> 00:15:52.610
models because they
are low-depth models,

00:15:52.611 --> 00:15:55.070
and they are designed not to

00:15:55.071 --> 00:15:58.390
be working in the ensemble form.

00:15:58.391 --> 00:16:00.570
But in case of decision trees
or random forest,

00:16:00.571 --> 00:16:04.150
even in random forest
it's requiring 250 random

00:16:04.151 --> 00:16:08.895
as in the ensembles to pick
up those weak classifiers.

00:16:08.896 --> 00:16:12.300
Which means that most
of the classifiers are

00:16:12.301 --> 00:16:13.890
actually picking
the strong signal

00:16:13.891 --> 00:16:16.180
and not the weak signals
in some sense.

00:16:16.181 --> 00:16:18.570
So, these are some next steps

00:16:18.571 --> 00:16:19.820
on all these three problems.

00:16:19.821 --> 00:16:20.855
On the first problem,

00:16:20.856 --> 00:16:23.240
I think we would evaluate Bonsai

00:16:23.241 --> 00:16:25.860
and ProtoNN on more datasets,

00:16:25.861 --> 00:16:27.340
and we would be putting

00:16:27.341 --> 00:16:29.315
it in deployment for
the pilot sites.

00:16:29.316 --> 00:16:33.000
For the preventive maintenance
case, we have tried,

00:16:33.001 --> 00:16:36.570
basically on one
particular manufacturer,

00:16:36.571 --> 00:16:37.740
so we want to replicate and

00:16:37.741 --> 00:16:39.040
see if the similar
type of features

00:16:39.041 --> 00:16:41.665
are applicable in
other manufacturers models.

00:16:41.666 --> 00:16:44.950
Also, we wanted to see
that for doing a weighing

00:16:44.951 --> 00:16:48.145
of downtime base on the- So,

00:16:48.146 --> 00:16:49.300
there are certain downtimes which

00:16:49.301 --> 00:16:50.435
are 10 minutes downtime,

00:16:50.436 --> 00:16:51.770
there are certain
downtimes which are

00:16:51.771 --> 00:16:53.405
like two hours or
four hours of downtime.

00:16:53.406 --> 00:16:55.905
So, can the predictor
do the weighing of

00:16:55.906 --> 00:16:59.255
downtimes based on the
amount of impact it creates?

00:16:59.256 --> 00:17:01.570
Also, increased the focus window.

00:17:01.571 --> 00:17:03.720
So, right now, we are
looking at two to four hours

00:17:03.721 --> 00:17:06.735
forecast window and we
increase it to one day.

00:17:06.736 --> 00:17:08.530
Does it make sense?
Does it really pick

00:17:08.531 --> 00:17:10.540
up the signal day
ahead in advance?

00:17:10.541 --> 00:17:11.300
So, those are the kind of

00:17:11.301 --> 00:17:12.760
things that we wanted to look at.

00:17:12.761 --> 00:17:14.730
In auto cleaning control,

00:17:14.731 --> 00:17:17.020
we would like to continue
with the evaluation of

00:17:17.021 --> 00:17:21.310
ProtoNN to see if for
these kind of problems,

00:17:21.311 --> 00:17:24.475
if ProtoNN would be able to
has multiple prototypes.

00:17:24.476 --> 00:17:26.100
So, if those
prototypes can pick up

00:17:26.101 --> 00:17:29.760
those weak points
or in some sense,

00:17:29.761 --> 00:17:31.760
anomalies or outliers which

00:17:31.761 --> 00:17:33.305
are already present in the data.

00:17:33.306 --> 00:17:37.160
So, those are the next steps
that we would like to try.

00:17:37.161 --> 00:17:38.310
I would like to thank you for

00:17:38.311 --> 00:17:43.835
the entire support from
different teams in MRS, Harsha,

00:17:43.836 --> 00:17:46.715
Prateek, Naga, Vivek, Aditya,

00:17:46.716 --> 00:17:49.065
all of them have helped
significantly in improving

00:17:49.066 --> 00:17:52.310
and trying out various approaches
where it's not working.

00:17:52.311 --> 00:17:54.310
We would like to continue
with the collaboration

00:17:54.311 --> 00:17:56.840
in future as well. Thank you.

00:18:01.590 --> 00:18:03.830
&gt;&gt; So, I have a question.
It seems that most of

00:18:03.831 --> 00:18:05.735
the things they're doing
are actually pretty

00:18:05.736 --> 00:18:07.970
I wouldn't say simple but

00:18:07.971 --> 00:18:10.230
pretty straightforward
time series,

00:18:10.231 --> 00:18:11.820
signal processing kind of task.

00:18:11.821 --> 00:18:14.140
For which, I feel like
machine learning type of thing,

00:18:14.141 --> 00:18:16.395
just like Jammer you're
throwing at this problem.

00:18:16.396 --> 00:18:18.610
Seem like classical, I'm not
a signal processing person

00:18:18.611 --> 00:18:19.790
but if I think of classical

00:18:19.791 --> 00:18:20.810
signal processing techniques for

00:18:20.811 --> 00:18:23.290
estimation detection should
be much more compact and

00:18:23.291 --> 00:18:26.030
much better than throwing
machine learning.

00:18:26.031 --> 00:18:27.730
So I don't know if
you've any thoughts

00:18:27.731 --> 00:18:29.570
about those kinds of things.

00:18:29.571 --> 00:18:30.980
It might actually get
a much complicated

00:18:30.981 --> 00:18:32.720
because you know it's
a time series problem,

00:18:32.721 --> 00:18:34.110
you know the time
series signature

00:18:34.111 --> 00:18:35.260
of what the pattern looks like.

00:18:35.261 --> 00:18:36.370
Say, I'm looking for
a square, we're or

00:18:36.371 --> 00:18:38.260
looking for a sine wave.

00:18:38.261 --> 00:18:41.755
Well, I'm not expert in
it, but I'm pretty sure,

00:18:41.756 --> 00:18:43.480
you ask signal processing,
then I'll say, "Okay,

00:18:43.481 --> 00:18:44.995
this is a carbon filter block,

00:18:44.996 --> 00:18:46.785
or match filter or something."

00:18:46.786 --> 00:18:49.775
So, I'm not sure why
machine learning for this.

00:18:49.776 --> 00:18:52.100
&gt;&gt; So, we have looked

00:18:52.101 --> 00:18:54.565
at few approaches for
signal processing.

00:18:54.566 --> 00:18:57.910
I think one thing
is in this entire,

00:18:57.911 --> 00:18:59.875
the way the equipments
are working,

00:18:59.876 --> 00:19:01.395
the signatures are not

00:19:01.396 --> 00:19:04.060
common across all
different manufacturers.

00:19:04.061 --> 00:19:06.575
Signatures are not-
so the kind of

00:19:06.576 --> 00:19:07.760
signatures which we are looking

00:19:07.761 --> 00:19:08.780
into this residual patterns,

00:19:08.781 --> 00:19:09.900
so those are the signatures which

00:19:09.901 --> 00:19:11.070
we are doing post-processing and

00:19:11.071 --> 00:19:13.650
based on the analysis that
we have done from the data,

00:19:13.651 --> 00:19:15.310
we are now identifying those are

00:19:15.311 --> 00:19:17.500
the processing
which are required.

00:19:17.501 --> 00:19:22.490
So, having a common generic model

00:19:22.491 --> 00:19:24.280
which is applicable
for different types of

00:19:24.281 --> 00:19:27.000
equipment and different
manufactures' equipment,

00:19:27.001 --> 00:19:29.285
I think is a challenging
task over here,

00:19:29.286 --> 00:19:31.180
and that's where
machine learning approaches

00:19:31.181 --> 00:19:32.670
like this coming in this.

00:19:32.671 --> 00:19:34.100
&gt;&gt; So, I guess there
is two parts of this.

00:19:34.101 --> 00:19:35.210
One is actually is talking

00:19:35.211 --> 00:19:37.550
to the students working
on the similar thing.

00:19:37.551 --> 00:19:38.720
I think there's
two parts of it. One is

00:19:38.721 --> 00:19:40.430
the signature synthesis for

00:19:40.431 --> 00:19:42.140
which machine learning might
be the right approach.

00:19:42.141 --> 00:19:43.660
Then there's the actual matching

00:19:43.661 --> 00:19:44.780
which you want run at the edge.

00:19:44.781 --> 00:19:45.920
Doing machine learning at

00:19:45.921 --> 00:19:47.190
the edge may not
make sense for this,

00:19:47.191 --> 00:19:48.690
you actually do
the complex machine learning

00:19:48.691 --> 00:19:50.305
to run a time series signature.

00:19:50.306 --> 00:19:51.740
You may actually want to split

00:19:51.741 --> 00:19:53.110
a board like an
offline-online process.

00:19:53.111 --> 00:19:54.905
Like what runs online might
not be machine learning,

00:19:54.906 --> 00:19:56.605
what runs offline might
be machine learning.

00:19:56.606 --> 00:19:58.160
Maybe that's
a better architecture

00:19:58.161 --> 00:19:59.410
for some of these applications.

00:19:59.411 --> 00:20:00.945
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, I think definitely

00:20:00.946 --> 00:20:02.990
I would like to evaluate
those approaches.

00:20:02.991 --> 00:20:04.190
But even in this case,

00:20:04.191 --> 00:20:06.630
that is the baby you're
trying to segregate it.

00:20:06.631 --> 00:20:09.080
So, the kind of
complex tasks which require

00:20:09.081 --> 00:20:11.170
additional third-party
information as well

00:20:11.171 --> 00:20:13.340
as if you start looking
at the head forecasting,

00:20:13.341 --> 00:20:15.485
we don't want to push the head
forecasting on the edge

00:20:15.486 --> 00:20:18.030
because that requires multiple
other sources information,

00:20:18.031 --> 00:20:21.020
and looks at much larger
amount of data from

00:20:21.021 --> 00:20:24.375
multiple years and
seasonalities and other things.

00:20:24.376 --> 00:20:25.950
So, that information,
those are the kinds

00:20:25.951 --> 00:20:27.770
of things which are
computed in Cloud.

00:20:27.771 --> 00:20:31.210
But when we finally push
that information on the edge,

00:20:31.211 --> 00:20:33.470
the issue is that we
need to make the signal,

00:20:33.471 --> 00:20:36.520
make this inference
every few minutes,

00:20:36.521 --> 00:20:38.465
or every five minutes,
every 15 minutes.

00:20:38.466 --> 00:20:41.855
At that time, we have
to otherwise call,

00:20:41.856 --> 00:20:43.880
if we're using
machine learning models,

00:20:43.881 --> 00:20:44.910
we have to call back to

00:20:44.911 --> 00:20:46.330
the Cloud and get
that information.

00:20:46.331 --> 00:20:47.600
So, we're trying to separate out

00:20:47.601 --> 00:20:48.865
the problem in the sense that

00:20:48.866 --> 00:20:50.560
whatever requires

00:20:50.561 --> 00:20:52.595
additional information
and larger computation,

00:20:52.596 --> 00:20:53.770
that's pushed into the cloud,

00:20:53.771 --> 00:20:55.310
whereas a very small problem,

00:20:55.311 --> 00:20:57.220
even in case of
optimization we do

00:20:57.221 --> 00:20:59.710
a head optimization based on
the day ahead information,

00:20:59.711 --> 00:21:01.010
we do the optimization, and

00:21:01.011 --> 00:21:03.465
only optimization revision
works on the edge.

00:21:03.466 --> 00:21:06.390
So in some societies,

00:21:06.391 --> 00:21:09.100
the classification of what
would run in the Cloud,

00:21:09.101 --> 00:21:11.735
but I think I would
need to look into,

00:21:11.736 --> 00:21:14.280
if there are other
similar processing kind

00:21:14.281 --> 00:21:17.360
of techniques which would
be helpful for this and

00:21:17.361 --> 00:21:24.260
not looked into that.

00:21:24.261 --> 00:21:25.040
&gt;&gt; Okay. Thank you for your time.

00:21:25.041 --> 00:21:26.580
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Thanks.

